Allocate capture buffers from a DMA-BUF heap

Every VA surface used to be a V4L2 MMAP buffer owned by the CAPTURE queue,
named by its index, mapped with mmap and exported with EXPBUF. The queue
owned the pixels, so it could not be released or renegotiated while any
surface was still referenced: a client creating surfaces at a new size or
depth with the old ones alive got EBUSY from S_FMT and fell back to
software. ffmpeg does exactly that on a VP9 or HEVC resolution change
(measured 2026-10-01 with ffmpeg 7.1.5).

Now each surface owns its memory. Its buffer comes from /dev/dma_heap/system
and is queued as V4L2_MEMORY_DMABUF, always with the same fd at the same
index, so vb2 keeps the mapping across decodes. Both decoders accept DMABUF
capture, and the system heap is right for them: the VE, the AV1 core and the
display are all IOMMU masters, so scattered pages are contiguous in each
device's address space (heap buffers on the H713 video plane were checked on
the panel in NV12, P010 and P010+LSB10 before this was written), and the
128 MB CMA pool could not hold a 4K surface pool anyway.

- Capture memory is chosen per device whenever its queue is configured from
  empty: DMABUF if the heap opened and the format is one buffer, otherwise
  MMAP, which is the previous behaviour. V4L2_REQUEST_CAPTURE_MEMORY=mmap or
  dmabuf forces either for A/B runs; a forced dmabuf that cannot be honoured
  fails instead of quietly running the other one. The choice is logged.
- Each heap buffer is sizeimage bytes, so it covers whatever the driver
  keeps behind the picture.
- Export (vaExportSurfaceHandle, vaAcquireBufferHandle) hands out a
  duplicate of the surface's fd instead of calling EXPBUF.
- CPU reads (vaDeriveImage, vaGetImage) are bracketed with
  DMA_BUF_IOCTL_SYNC: heap memory is cached and vb2 no longer does that
  maintenance.
- OUTPUT (bitstream) buffers stay MMAP.

No behaviour change is intended by this patch alone: the queues are still
held while surfaces exist (0009). Renegotiating while old surfaces are
alive is the next patch.

